<section
  id='case-portfolio'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: Personal Portfolio | Web Development | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='A custom portfolio built with Handlebars, modular JavaScript, client-side routing, responsive images, theme switching, and accessible GSAP animation.'
  data-case='portfolio'
  aria-labelledby='case-portfolio-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Web Development</p>
        <h1 id='case-portfolio-title'>Katja Dev Portfolio</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>
          A custom portfolio that brings my design work, front-end projects, and visual identity
          together.
        </p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>UX/UI design and front-end development</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Independent portfolio project</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2025–2026</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Handlebars, GSAP, Vite</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Live portfolio and evolving codebase</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='portfolio'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='Katja Dev Portfolio sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='portfolio'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        I wanted the portfolio to show how I work across design and code, rather than simply placing
        finished projects inside a template. It needed room for development, interaction design, and
        visual design case studies, each with its own content and links. The site also had to feel
        personal without allowing the visual effects to compete with the work.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            The site uses animated backgrounds, page transitions, theme switching, and fullscreen
            navigation. Those features share the same interface and can easily interfere with one
            another if their states are handled separately. I also needed a structure that could
            support new projects without duplicating routes, navigation links, and project metadata
            throughout the codebase.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Give the site a strong visual identity without reducing readability</li>
            <li>Keep routes, project links, and active navigation states in sync</li>
            <li>Control the cost of animation and large imagery on smaller devices</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--desktop'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-portfolio-600.webp'
            data-src='site-portfolio-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='400'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Katja Dev Portfolio desktop homepage with typography, work navigation, and animated visual elements'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Desktop homepage and visual identity</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I divided the interface into Handlebars partials and focused JavaScript modules.
              Routing, navigation, themes, responsive images, and animation each have a defined
              responsibility, which makes changes easier to trace.
            </li>
            <li>
              Project data acts as a shared source for work cards, case-study switchers, and demo
              links. Adding or changing a project can update several parts of the site without
              repeating the same information in each view.
            </li>
            <li>
              GSAP controls the waves, page transitions, and liquid-inspired background blobs. The
              effects respond to interaction and scrolling, while reduced-motion preferences provide
              a calmer version of the experience.
            </li>
            <li>
              Responsive image handling selects a suitable asset for the available screen size.
              Heavier animation is deferred until the main interface is ready, which protects the
              first view from work that does not need to happen immediately.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--mobile'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-portfoliomobile-600.webp'
            data-src='site-portfoliomobile-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='490'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Katja Dev Portfolio mobile homepage and fullscreen navigation menu'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Responsive homepage and fullscreen menu</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The portfolio now works as both a presentation of my projects and a front-end project in its
        own right. Its visual character comes from my design background, while the code demonstrates
        modular JavaScript, client-side routing, reusable data, responsive assets, theme logic, and
        accessible motion. I keep refining it as the content grows. That process has made me more
        selective with animation. An effect earns its place when it supports the atmosphere or helps
        the interface feel responsive. If it distracts from the project itself, I simplify it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'>
          <use href='#i-arrow-left' />
        </svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>